This notice carries two facts about what this software does and does not
guarantee. Apache-2.0 §4(d) requires them to travel with any redistribution,
which is why they are here rather than only in the documentation.

1. THE CREDENTIAL THIS SOFTWARE HOLDS CAN TRADE.

   Robinhood advertises a single OAuth scope, `internal`. There is no
   read-only scope to request. A token obtained by this software is capable of
   placing, cancelling, and exercising orders, whatever this software chooses
   to do with it.

   What restrains it is a human-reviewed manifest committed to this package,
   plus exact digest comparisons made before every call. That boundary is
   "no trading", not "no writes": the reviewed manifest denies all six order
   tools and both order simulators, and allows a set of non-trading mutations
   (watchlist and saved-scan management) alongside its reads. Each capability
   carries a reviewed `mutates` flag.

   A fork that edits the manifest, weakens the digest checks, or ships its own
   manifest changes this property. Apache-2.0 §4(b) requires such a fork to
   carry prominent notice of the modification. For this software that
   requirement is not a formality — the manifest is the entire security
   boundary, and a consumer pinning a digest is relying on it.

2. THIS SOFTWARE HAS HAD ONE INDEPENDENT SECURITY REVIEW. IT FOUND BLOCKING
   DEFECTS, AND IT COVERS VERSION 0.1.0 ONLY.

   Its own design document (DESIGN.md §12) lists an independent review as a
   release-acceptance requirement. That requirement was waived for 0.1.0. It
   has since been met: an AI-assisted reviewer outside this project examined
   the exact released 0.1.0 artifacts and returned CHANGES_REQUIRED. The full
   report and the reviewer's adversarial tests are committed in this
   repository at `security-review/v0.1.0/`.

   The two blocking findings, both fixed in 0.2.0:

     - A public export in `rh_mcp.transport` accepted an arbitrary provider
       tool name with no manifest check. The reviewer reached
       `place_equity_order` through it, while the documentation stated no
       public surface could do that.
     - Argument validation ran against a private copy and the result was
       discarded; the caller's live mapping was sent to the provider, so
       undeclared keys could be added after validation passed.

   Four earlier review rounds found neither. Those rounds were conducted by
   agents operating under the same orchestration as the implementation, and
   they did find real defects — a fail-open argument-validation bypass, a
   callback-abort bypass, a credential record reaching a public repr, an
   account identifier reaching consumer-visible JSON — each fixed and covered
   by tests. They were not independent in the sense §12 means, and the two
   findings above are the evidence that the distinction is not procedural.

   What this review is not: a human penetration test, a third-party
   certification, or a live-authenticated assessment. The reviewer disclosed
   that it ran in a cloud-agent environment previously used for this
   repository's development setup. Its approval gate is bound to the exact
   0.1.0 commit and artifacts it names. VERSION 0.2.0 HAS NOT BEEN
   RE-REVIEWED.

   Anyone deploying this against a real brokerage account should read that as
   what it is.

